#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148482} :activity/id #uuid "e30f8e58-bf46-5a3b-b6e5-78f40e3c48c1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049531 :topic/id #uuid "fc9ea30d-c77e-520f-8dab-e177eee4a483" :topic/math-academy-id 2457} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148698 :tutorial/content "When working with fractions, percents, and decimals, we often need to decide which number is greater or smaller. Since these numbers are written in different forms, it can be difficult to compare them directly.\n\nTo make comparisons easier, we can *convert them to a common form*.\n\nFor example, let's compare the percent $48\\%$ and the decimal $0.52$.\n\nOne way to do that is to convert the percent to a decimal. So, we divide the percent value by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n48 \\div 100 = 0.48\n$$\n\nThus, $48\\%$ is equivalent to $0.48$.\n\nNow, we can compare the two decimals:\n$0.48 < 0.52$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $48\\%$ is smaller than $0.52$.\n\nLet's see more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "e6681afa-a432-5074-a46c-204561f2cf65" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2078 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d9c7592c-10c0-5cc3-b1ff-b5b67dfe08ba" :step/math-academy-id 632742 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148483}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147542 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147245 :question/id #uuid "7dd3b106-ac45-522a-97ea-6dc4355f0247" :question/math-academy-id "e-2317"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6be40082-db16-5983-aa43-9b6ee8f59def" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050030}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147796}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Decimal to a Percent"} :step/id #uuid "5df02fb6-3576-5996-8b1e-64978fa7b5d8" :step/math-academy-id 632743 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147543 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147246 :question/id #uuid "22bfefb3-89b4-5d42-b305-732790c89fbd" :question/math-academy-id "e-2318"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37060efd-560b-5d57-b108-07c202e3abcc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050478}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434387}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Percent to a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "f6835341-5e27-57e3-ae67-a97b6df1ba75" :step/math-academy-id 632746 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148670 :tutorial/content "Suppose Ethan and Ryan are baseball players. In the previous season, $24\\%$ of Ethan’s hits were home runs, while the decimal proportion of Ryan’s hits that were home runs was $0.3$.\n\nLet’s determine whether either Ethan or Ryan hit home runs on more than half of their total hits.\n\n- *For Ethan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a percentage, $50\\%$. Since\n$24\\% < 50\\%$,\nless than half of Ethan’s hits were home runs.\n- *For Ryan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a decimal, $0.5$. Since\n$0.3 < 0.5$,\nless than half of Ryan’s hits were home runs.\n\nTherefore, neither player hit home runs on more than half of their hits.\n\nNow, let’s see more concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "fbb4d977-7db5-579e-baf0-bb8e13b6c15a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14935 :tutorial/title "Comparing Decimals and Percents in Context"} :step/id #uuid "4ce04142-5c86-5b4c-a76f-55af88901484" :step/math-academy-id 632749 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148486}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147544 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147235 :question/id #uuid "4016fd3c-d8b2-5ef2-8554-47ac5c183950" :question/math-academy-id "e-22017"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "638e604b-5416-5d3c-a61f-5be645aa255b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434388}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements in Context"} :step/id #uuid "c0e51654-69d7-5097-b27f-71f3c28bce97" :step/math-academy-id 632750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148671 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose Carlos and Henry have the same number of trading cards. A total of $32 \\%$ of Carlos's cards are rare cards, while\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nof Henry's cards are rare cards.\n\nWho has more rare cards?\n\nTo answer this, we need to *compare the fraction and the percentage*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 32 \\%\n$$\n\nA common method is to convert the fraction to a percentage.\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nas an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 25}{4 \\times 25} = \\dfrac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{100} = 25 \\%\n$$\n\nSince $25 \\% < 32 \\%$, we can conclude that Carlos has *more* rare cards than Carlos." :tutorial/id #uuid "4be68676-8fcc-56ce-a8ef-96b2a954a03c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14937 :tutorial/title "Comparing Fractions and Percents in Context"} :step/id #uuid "99330381-c770-5a62-a3c7-a79ba13fcd0b" :step/math-academy-id 632753 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147545 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147247 :question/id #uuid "e6b550b1-cd66-5a70-9d11-d37733ac4c5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2319"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d39373d-ba41-53aa-8119-4ecf5138a50a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424830}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Fractions and Percents: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "5102e680-a474-5349-a6c9-27bc67303b7a" :step/math-academy-id 632754}] :activity/title "Comparing Fractions, Percents, and Decimals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147658}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434389} :activity/id #uuid "8fdc0e2d-e98a-546b-920a-d66ad029459e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049531 :topic/id #uuid "fc9ea30d-c77e-520f-8dab-e177eee4a483" :topic/math-academy-id 2457} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434389 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3eaa45b-e95e-56ec-8a67-73501143f470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "greater than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144527} {:answer/id #uuid "07152769-74bf-591a-aebb-ca667326355d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "smaller than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144528} {:answer/id #uuid "159f1862-a910-5b33-adae-2450e3202d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "equal to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144528} :answer-field/id #uuid "43928eed-5b05-5d51-9f93-ead6691909e7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144526} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46fc5334-0c6a-5482-b87a-7c4318f09d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144531} {:answer/id #uuid "474395f7-2d91-5a70-a4d4-165bc3b7a704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144532} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9e9564-165a-5562-94b1-894dc7afe170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144533} {:answer/id #uuid "7308d008-65ca-5910-a2b3-b1bc12fd93af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144534}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144533} :answer-field/id #uuid "1809e6b8-eb6a-53bc-901a-eef9d1d4b809" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a5c264b-c2e7-5576-92a3-b7dd68b10806" :question/math-academy-id "q-348936" :question/problem "Select the options that make the statement below true.\n\n$\\,$\n$20%$ is equivalent to \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is \n{{field-2}}\n$\\frac{2}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert the percent to a fraction, we first write $20%$ as a fraction with a denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n20%=\\frac{20}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{100}=\\frac{20÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{2}{10}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{10}=\\frac{2÷2}{10÷2}=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}<\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $20%$ is equivalent to $\\frac{1}{5},$ \nwhich is $\\text{smaller than}$ $\\frac{2}{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "334dd9af-1647-5c57-bfb3-97a4e3ad59e7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434392}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f08fc0-fe2a-50b1-9757-0125a843874f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144561} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee410c9-3598-5305-9b67-6d7874ec6987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144562} {:answer/id #uuid "617a7101-40ff-5835-8af1-774698d53116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144563} {:answer/id #uuid "69438504-11e1-507e-aec0-5a2eb343486b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144564} {:answer/id #uuid "77799f28-8b1d-514a-af66-5e9854758919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144562} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0b76fa2-8ce2-54d1-84d0-0c484f99e832" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "436201f3-78ea-5cda-b175-c5331e2f77ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-348980" :question/problem "Lionel and David are youth soccer players. In the previous season, $12%$ of Lionel’s goals were scored from the penalty spot, while the decimal proportion of David’s goals scored from the penalty spot was $0.1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than half of Lionel's goals scored were from the penalty spot.\n\nII. More than half of David's goals scored were from the penalty spot.\n\nIII. Lionel had a greater percentage of goals scored from the penalty spot compared to David." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n12%<50%,\n$$\n\nless than half of Lionel's goals scored were from the penalty spot.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n0.1<0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of David's goals scored were from the penalty spot.\n\n- Statement III is true. We can compare $12%$ and $0.1$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.1\\times 100=10%\n$$\n\nSince  $12%>10%,$ Lionel had a **greater** percentage of goals scored from the penalty spot compared to David.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "37767ed4-b4c6-547e-a639-f6aeaee3ddec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434391}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fc398c3-ef25-52ba-8a15-a914c2acb08b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "equal to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434378} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6ea343-a5aa-5d69-b351-d0c27cab6e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "greater than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434382} {:answer/id #uuid "f52cda3d-118c-5960-b7d2-b5e6e6485224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "smaller than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434383}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434382} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b435f79-e45f-54bc-a9c1-2ebc68f39df2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434385} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355f6139-1632-5fb9-a099-f60c0204143d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434373} {:answer/id #uuid "43eb7fb9-45e1-5182-81e4-f59709d8a423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434374} {:answer/id #uuid "4da1f349-69e6-5209-8396-1c722ffbf3d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434375} {:answer/id #uuid "4e451e41-9d76-5570-9fd1-85be78bfe38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434376}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434375} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1896846-f13a-5c11-a37a-ffc9a894ca42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "138791bc-5cad-58ca-ae3d-64015f9ea134" :question/math-academy-id "q-349678" :question/problem "Select the options that make the statement below true.\n\n$\\,$\n$45%$ is equivalent to \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is \n{{field-2}}\n$\\frac{17}{40}.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert the percent to a fraction, we first write $45%$ as a fraction with a denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n45%=\\frac{45}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=\\frac{45÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can write this with a denominator of $40$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{20}=\\frac{9\\times 2}{20\\times 2}=\\frac{18}{40}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{40}>\\frac{17}{40}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45%$ is equivalent to $\\frac{18}{40},$ \nwhich is $\\text{greater than}$ $\\frac{17}{40}.$"} :step/id #uuid "63533d39-a10e-52e0-8ad1-5274656cd410" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a67e790c-f268-5a24-94e8-ca5c04dedf83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144545} {:answer/id #uuid "c936d84b-d07f-525b-9bc9-6ff7f01e4d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144546} {:answer/id #uuid "eadc4352-8c2c-59f9-9473-b7ec2bfbcf10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "75%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144547} {:answer/id #uuid "3b38da8f-941c-53fd-91b1-d2edd954e611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "34%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144548}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144547} :answer-field/id #uuid "2653229a-ec4a-5e31-8fa9-9eb366c71a49" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144544} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e538c8b1-a7d7-56df-8afa-ab47b7820cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "fewer" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144550} {:answer/id #uuid "58139854-a8fc-5365-b0a4-5f76bb20339b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "more" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144551} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4996420-521b-5b75-b05e-f70a30820317" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "57efd73d-0f59-56f1-a156-7f1ee9e1fa2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-348969" :question/problem "Matt and Abraham have the same number of marbles. A total of $63%$ of Matt's marbles are blue, while $\\frac{3}{4}$ of Abraham's marbles are blue. Select the options that make the statements below true.\n\n$\\,$\nA total of \n{{field-1}}\nof Abraham's marbles are blue. \n\n$\\,$\nTherefore, he has \n{{field-2}}\nblue marbles compared to Matt." :question/worked-solution "We need to compare $\\frac{3}{4}$ with $63%.$ We can do this by writing $\\frac{3}{4}$ as a percent.\n\nFirst, we write $\\frac{3}{4}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{75}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{75}{100}=75%\n$$\n\nSince $75%>63%,$ we have the following:\n\n- A total of $75%$ of Abraham's marbles are blue.\n\n- Therefore, he has $\\text{more}$ blue marbles compared to Matt."} :step/id #uuid "cf9b1dbf-2308-5acb-ba12-635c8825ef8b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1129c1e9-1f22-5780-9696-b4a255e78851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434372} {:answer/id #uuid "585c654e-1a6c-5f56-ac8d-5f161ee40bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434377} {:answer/id #uuid "739f06d6-c702-56c9-a051-08e032332d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434379} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab8acb2-0f16-5f40-8fd6-1f3209f135e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434380} {:answer/id #uuid "c956c11a-2fd4-59e8-a085-706468c3a376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434381}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434381} :answer-field/id #uuid "09954eee-e3cc-5394-bd09-4440f9d978a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "16a4ed86-6ba4-5b08-b5bc-00c64da3da49" :question/math-academy-id "q-350266" :question/problem "Sana and Talia are students. In the previous semester, the decimal proportion of Sana’s homework assignments that were turned in early was $0.4,$ while $45%$ of Talia’s homework assignments were turned in early. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\nII. More than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\nIII. Sana had a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n0.4<0.5,\n$$\n\nless than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n45%<50%.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement III is false. We can compare $0.4$ and $45%$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.4\\times 100=40%\n$$\n\nSince  $40%<45%,$ Sana did not have a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "e00df753-1591-526d-a3e8-f3c5207c5bfd"}] :activity/title "Comparing Fractions, Percents, and Decimals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434371}]}
